<!-- ============ CASH ============ -->
<section class="lineup-section" id="cash">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="lineup-tag tag-cash">Cash Game Build</span>
    <h2>Week 2 Cash Lineup for DraftKings Sun 9/20 1 PM Slate</h2>
    <p class="lineup-excerpt">A near-maxed-out, all-CORE build — 8 of 9 roster spots grade out as CORE plays, with Kayshon Boutte's $4,000 price tag the single best value on the entire WR board.</p>
    <div class="lineup-stats-row">
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">$49,000</span><span class="l">Salary Used</span></div>
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">178.03</span><span class="l">Proj. Total</span></div>
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">8 / 9</span><span class="l">CORE-Grade Spots</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <h3>Definition</h3>
      <p><strong>Cash is the highest-floor, most optimal build, and the one official cash lineup Slate-IQ enters every week.</strong> It gets 4 of the week's 15 $1 entries, all placed into 50/50s and Double-Ups, where roughly half the field cashes and everyone who does gets paid the same. The build skews heavily toward the model's highest-confidence tier (75–85% core): one primary QB, high-floor RBs and WRs, a safe TE, and a strong DST — no stacking, no ownership consideration, just the most efficient roster available. It's also the fixed benchmark every other lineup gets measured against each week.</p>
      <h3>Why This Lineup, Why Now</h3>
      <p>This week's Cash build is about as clean as the model produces: 8 of the 9 roster spots grade out as Grade A/CORE, and the one exception (Dalton Schultz at TE) is still a Signal VALUE play, not a WATCH-tier question mark. There's no roster spot here that requires a leap of faith — every player is either the model's top or near-top option at their position on pure production, efficiency, or both.</p>
      <p>The headline is Kayshon Boutte at $4,000. He projects for 28.55 points — 1st among all 39 active WRs in both raw points and value this week, ahead of players priced nearly double his salary — which is the single biggest edge on this week's board and the main reason $1,000 of cap space is still sitting unused even after rostering three $7,000+ pieces (Bijan Robinson, Derrick Henry, Chris Olave).</p>
      <h3>The Lineup, Player by Player</h3>
      <p><strong>QB — Trevor Lawrence ($5,800, JAX vs. DEN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 22.15 points — 2nd among 25 active QBs in raw points and 1st in value. Jacksonville is favored by 2.5 at home in a 44.5-point game, for a 21.0 implied team total — a positive script without needing to lean on garbage-time volume, and the cheapest of the week's true CORE-tier QBs.</p>
      <p><strong>RB — Bijan Robinson ($8,200, ATL vs. CAR)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 25.16 points — 1st among 35 active RBs in raw points, 3rd in value. The most expensive player in the lineup and worth every dollar of it — no real competition for touches in Atlanta's backfield, in a game Atlanta is favored to control, for a 21.0 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>RB — Derrick Henry ($7,200, BAL vs. NO)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 23.10 points — 2nd among RBs in raw points, 1st in value. Baltimore's 8.5-point favorite role gives him the clearest touchdown equity of any back on the slate — the best cash-and-ceiling profile at either RB spot, for a 28.0 implied team total, the highest on this roster.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Chris Olave ($7,200, NO vs. BAL)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 20.88 points — 2nd among 39 active WRs in raw points, 5th in value. New Orleans' clear WR1 in what should be a high-volume role as a sizable underdog (19.5 implied total) — a big underdog script tends to mean more targets, not fewer, for a team's top receiver.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Christian Watson ($6,200, GB vs. NYJ)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 19.08 points — 3rd among WRs in raw points and value alike. Green Bay is favored by 3.5 at home, for a 24.0 implied team total — a stable, positive-script role without needing anything unusual to happen.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Kayshon Boutte ($4,000, HOU vs. CIN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 28.55 points — 1st among all 39 active WRs in both raw points and value, at the cheapest salary of any player in this lineup. This is the build's defining edge: a $4,000 price tag attached to the single best point total at the position on the entire board, for a 24.75 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>TE — Dallas Goedert ($4,800, PHI vs. TEN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 15.39 points — 2nd among 27 active TEs in raw points and value. Philadelphia is favored by 7, the largest spread on either side of its own matchup — a strong game-script signal for a receiving TE, for a 23.25 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>FLEX — Dalton Schultz ($3,200, HOU vs. CIN)</strong><br>Grade B, VALUE signal, projected for 14.35 points — 3rd among TEs in raw points, 1st in value. The one non-CORE spot on the roster, and still a clean, defensible hold — cheapest piece in the lineup, and the second Houston player alongside Boutte in the same CIN@HOU game (24.75 implied total).</p>
      <p><strong>DST — Jacksonville Jaguars ($2,400, JAX vs. DEN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 9.38 points — 1st among 26 active DSTs in both raw points and value. Jacksonville's own game environment (44.5 total, 2.5-point favorite, 21.0 implied total) supports the pick independent of Lawrence being rostered above it — this isn't a forced same-team pairing, both grade out as the position's best options on their own merits.</p>
      <h3>The Full Roster</h3>
      <div class="roster-wrap">
        <table class="roster-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Pos</th><th>Player</th><th>Salary</th><th>Matchup</th><th>Vegas (Total/Spread)</th><th>Implied Total</th><th>Proj. DK</th><th>Value ($/1K)</th><th>Grade</th><th>Signal</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="pos">QB</td><td class="name">Trevor Lawrence</td><td>$5,800</td><td>JAX vs. DEN</td><td>44.5 / +2.5</td><td>21.0</td><td>22.15</td><td>3.82</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">RB</td><td class="name">Bijan Robinson</td><td>$8,200</td><td>ATL vs. CAR</td><td>43.5 / +1.5</td><td>21.0</td><td>25.16</td><td>3.07</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">RB</td><td class="name">Derrick Henry</td><td>$7,200</td><td>BAL vs. NO</td><td>47.5 / -8.5</td><td>28.0</td><td>23.10</td><td>3.21</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td class="name">Chris Olave</td><td>$7,200</td><td>NO vs. BAL</td><td>47.5 / +8.5</td><td>19.5</td><td>20.88</td><td>2.90</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td class="name">Christian Watson</td><td>$6,200</td><td>GB vs. NYJ</td><td>44.5 / -3.5</td><td>24.0</td><td>19.08</td><td>3.08</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td class="name">Kayshon Boutte</td><td>$4,000</td><td>HOU vs. CIN</td><td>46.5 / -3.0</td><td>24.75</td><td>28.55</td><td>7.14</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">TE</td><td class="name">Dallas Goedert</td><td>$4,800</td><td>PHI vs. TEN</td><td>39.5 / -7.0</td><td>23.25</td><td>15.39</td><td>3.21</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">FLEX</td><td class="name">Dalton Schultz</td><td>$3,200</td><td>HOU vs. CIN</td><td>46.5 / -3.0</td><td>24.75</td><td>14.35</td><td>4.49</td><td class="grade">B</td><td class="signal-VALUE">VALUE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">DST</td><td class="name">Jaguars</td><td>$2,400</td><td>JAX vs. DEN</td><td>44.5 / +2.5</td><td>21.0</td><td>9.38</td><td>3.91</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr class="total"><td colspan="2">Total</td><td>$49,000</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>178.03</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p><em>Spread is shown from each listed player's own team perspective (negative = favorite). Implied team total = (Game Total − Team Spread) ÷ 2. "vs." labels the matchup only and doesn't indicate home/away. Injury blank = Active; none of this roster's players currently carry a Questionable/Doubtful/Out tag. Roster floor: ~123.68. Roster ceiling: ~250.18. $1,000 of cap space is unused.</em></p>
      <h3>What the Numbers Tell Us</h3>
      <p>There's a mild, incidental correlation footprint here even though Cash isn't built for it: Kayshon Boutte and Dalton Schultz are both Houston pieces in the same CIN@HOU game, and Trevor Lawrence and the Jaguars D/ST are both Jacksonville — though that pairing is common in Cash builds and reflects each grading out as the position's best option independently, not a stack decision. Otherwise this roster is spread across seven different games with no single environment doing disproportionate work. The more telling number is the value distribution: every non-Schultz starter clears a 2.9 value floor, and three players (Lawrence, Henry, Boutte) sit at or above 3.2 — this is a genuinely efficient roster, not just a high-projection one padded by a couple of expensive stars.</p>
      <h3>DFS Translation</h3>
      <p><strong>How to read Grade and Signal:</strong> Signal layers Value (projected points per $1,000 of salary) on top of Grade to flag roster fit:</p>
      <ul class="dfs-list">
        <li><strong>CORE</strong> — Grade A and Value at or above a position-specific bar. Build your lineup around this player.</li>
        <li><strong>VALUE</strong> — Salary at or below a position-specific ceiling and Value at or above a bar. A cheap, efficient pivot that frees up cap space.</li>
        <li><strong>WATCH</strong> — Neither bar was cleared. Not a red flag, just no strong edge either way.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The exact cutoffs differ by position — a WR needs fewer raw points than a QB to earn a Grade A, for example:</p>
      <div class="roster-wrap">
        <table class="roster-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Grade A</th><th>Grade B</th><th>CORE — Value &ge;</th><th>VALUE — Salary &le;</th><th>VALUE — Value percentile &ge;</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="pos">QB</td><td>&ge; 20</td><td>&ge; 16</td><td>2.95</td><td>$6,500</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">RB</td><td>&ge; 18</td><td>&ge; 12</td><td>2.5</td><td>$5,800</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td>&ge; 16</td><td>&ge; 11</td><td>2.4</td><td>$5,200</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">TE</td><td>&ge; 15</td><td>&ge; 10</td><td>2.5</td><td>$3,700</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">DST</td><td>&ge; 7.5</td><td>top 42% (58th pct)</td><td>2.4</td><td>$2,600</td><td>65th</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Below the Grade B line at each position is a Grade C; missing both the CORE and VALUE bars is a WATCH.</p>
      <p>For this build specifically:</p>
      <div class="translation-row">
        <div class="translation-pill core"><b>CORE (safest floor)</b>Trevor Lawrence, Bijan Robinson, Derrick Henry, Chris Olave, Christian Watson, Kayshon Boutte, Dallas Goedert, Jaguars D/ST</div>
        <div class="translation-pill value"><b>VALUE (good pts/$)</b>Dalton Schultz</div>
      </div>
      <p>Eight CORE, one VALUE, zero WATCH — the deepest CORE tilt of any Cash build this season, with $1,000 still on the table.</p>
      <h3>The Slate-IQ Take</h3>
      <p>There isn't much to hedge on this week's Cash build — zero WATCH-tier spots is rare, and even the one non-CORE piece (Schultz) is a clean VALUE play rather than a speculative one. If there's a single point worth flagging plainly, it's that three of the nine salaries (Bijan Robinson, Derrick Henry, Chris Olave) sit at $7,000+, which is what's actually driving the unused $1,000 of cap space — the model isn't leaving money on the table by accident, it's just that Boutte's $4,000 price tag is so far under his own production that there wasn't a better place to spend it without displacing a CORE play for a lesser one. That's a good problem to have in a Cash build.</p>
      <h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
      <p>This is about as clean a Cash build as the model produces this week — no WATCH-tier spots, one clear value outlier in Kayshon Boutte, and $1,000 still on the table because the model simply ran out of better places to put it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ BALANCED ============ -->
<section class="lineup-section" id="balanced">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="lineup-tag tag-balanced">Balanced Build</span>
    <h2>Week 2 Balanced Lineup for DraftKings Sun 9/20 1 PM Slate</h2>
    <p class="lineup-excerpt">A projection-driven build that ended up touching three separate games through seven of its nine spots — including a real Tyler Shough/Chris Olave same-team stack with Derrick Henry as the bring-back.</p>
    <div class="lineup-stats-row">
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">$49,700</span><span class="l">Salary Used</span></div>
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">164.71</span><span class="l">Proj. Total</span></div>
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">3</span><span class="l">Correlated Games</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <h3>Definition</h3>
      <p><strong>Balanced is the hybrid build, still projection-driven but with some correlation and a few lower-owned pivots.</strong> It gets 3 of the week's 15 $1 entries, going into Single-Entry-Only GPPs, a format built for exactly one well-considered lineup rather than volume. Construction-wise it stays close to Cash: the same primary QB (or the secondary one), with just 1–2 player swaps for higher-upside, lower-owned pieces — moderate leverage and some correlation, not a full stack.</p>
      <h3>Why This Lineup, Why Now</h3>
      <p>Worth being upfront about this build's shape before getting into the players: it carries more real correlation than a typical Balanced roster. Tyler Shough stacks with Chris Olave on the same New Orleans offense (both underdogs by 8.5 in a 47.5-point game), and Derrick Henry sits on the other side of that exact game as the bring-back — a genuine 3-piece, both-sides stack, not an incidental overlap. On top of that, two more pairs happen to share games: Chase Brown (CIN) and Kayshon Boutte (HOU) both touch the CIN@HOU game, and D'Andre Swift (CHI) and the Vikings D/ST (MIN) both touch CHI@MIN. Put together, 7 of the 9 roster spots touch a shared game with at least one teammate. That's a bigger correlation footprint than Balanced's usual "1-2 swaps, no real stack" shape — closer to what Leverage or Ceiling normally look like — but it's a byproduct of where this week's best individual plays and the model's own soft QB-stack preference landed, not a deliberate departure from the formula.</p>
      <h3>The Lineup, Player by Player</h3>
      <p><strong>QB — Tyler Shough ($5,300, NO vs. BAL)</strong><br>Grade B, VALUE signal, projected for 18.89 points — 6th among 25 active QBs in raw points, 4th in value. New Orleans is an 8.5-point underdog in a 47.5-point game, for a 19.5 implied team total — the exact kind of negative-script, pass-heavy setup that tends to inflate a young QB's volume, and it puts him in a natural stack with Olave below.</p>
      <p><strong>RB — Derrick Henry ($7,200, BAL vs. NO)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 23.10 points — 2nd among 35 active RBs in raw points, 1st in value. The bring-back half of this build's New Orleans-Baltimore correlation, and independently the best RB value on the entire board regardless of the stack, for a 28.0 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>RB — D'Andre Swift ($6,300, CHI vs. MIN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 20.09 points — 3rd among RBs in raw points and value. Chicago is favored by 5.5 in the week's 2nd-highest total game (49.5), for a 27.5 implied team total — a strong workload projection in a good scoring environment.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Chris Olave ($7,200, NO vs. BAL)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 20.88 points — 2nd among 39 active WRs in raw points, 5th in value. New Orleans' clear WR1 and Shough's direct stack partner — the centerpiece of this build's correlation thesis, for a 19.5 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Stefon Diggs ($5,300, WAS vs. DAL)</strong><br>Grade B, WATCH signal, projected for 13.81 points — 10th among WRs in raw points, 8th in value. Washington-Dallas carries the actual highest total on the entire Week 2 board (50.5), and Diggs is priced at just $5,300 for exposure to it, for a 23.5 implied team total — a game-environment play more than a per-target volume bet.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Kayshon Boutte ($4,000, HOU vs. CIN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 28.55 points — 1st among WRs in both raw points and value, for a 24.75 implied team total — the same league-best value that anchors this week's Cash build, now doing double duty here.</p>
      <p><strong>TE — Tucker Kraft ($4,900, GB vs. NYJ)</strong><br>Grade B, WATCH signal, projected for 13.93 points — 4th among 27 active TEs in raw points, 8th in value. Green Bay is favored by 3.5 at home, for a 24.0 implied team total — a solid game script without a standout individual signal at the position this week; a serviceable rather than exceptional roster spot.</p>
      <p><strong>FLEX — Chase Brown ($6,900, CIN vs. HOU)</strong><br>Grade B, WATCH signal, projected for 17.48 points — 6th among RBs in raw points, 9th in value. The Cincinnati side of this build's second incidental game overlap with Boutte, for a 21.75 implied team total — solid production without clearing either Signal bar this week.</p>
      <p><strong>DST — Minnesota Vikings ($2,600, MIN vs. CHI)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 8.00 points — 4th among 26 active DSTs in raw points, 3rd in value. The Minnesota side of this build's third incidental overlap, alongside D'Andre Swift's Chicago game (22.0 implied total) — a strong unit in its own right independent of that connection.</p>
      <h3>The Full Roster</h3>
      <div class="roster-wrap">
        <table class="roster-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Pos</th><th>Player</th><th>Salary</th><th>Matchup</th><th>Vegas (Total/Spread)</th><th>Implied Total</th><th>Proj. DK</th><th>Value ($/1K)</th><th>Grade</th><th>Signal</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="pos">QB</td><td class="name">Tyler Shough</td><td>$5,300</td><td>NO vs. BAL</td><td>47.5 / +8.5</td><td>19.5</td><td>18.89</td><td>3.56</td><td class="grade">B</td><td class="signal-VALUE">VALUE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">RB</td><td class="name">Derrick Henry</td><td>$7,200</td><td>BAL vs. NO</td><td>47.5 / -8.5</td><td>28.0</td><td>23.10</td><td>3.21</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">RB</td><td class="name">D'Andre Swift</td><td>$6,300</td><td>CHI vs. MIN</td><td>49.5 / -5.5</td><td>27.5</td><td>20.09</td><td>3.19</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td class="name">Chris Olave</td><td>$7,200</td><td>NO vs. BAL</td><td>47.5 / +8.5</td><td>19.5</td><td>20.88</td><td>2.90</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td class="name">Stefon Diggs</td><td>$5,300</td><td>WAS vs. DAL</td><td>50.5 / +3.5</td><td>23.5</td><td>13.81</td><td>2.61</td><td class="grade">B</td><td class="signal-WATCH">WATCH</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td class="name">Kayshon Boutte</td><td>$4,000</td><td>HOU vs. CIN</td><td>46.5 / -3.0</td><td>24.75</td><td>28.55</td><td>7.14</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">TE</td><td class="name">Tucker Kraft</td><td>$4,900</td><td>GB vs. NYJ</td><td>44.5 / -3.5</td><td>24.0</td><td>13.93</td><td>2.84</td><td class="grade">B</td><td class="signal-WATCH">WATCH</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">FLEX</td><td class="name">Chase Brown</td><td>$6,900</td><td>CIN vs. HOU</td><td>46.5 / +3.0</td><td>21.75</td><td>17.48</td><td>2.53</td><td class="grade">B</td><td class="signal-WATCH">WATCH</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">DST</td><td class="name">Vikings</td><td>$2,600</td><td>MIN vs. CHI</td><td>49.5 / +5.5</td><td>22.0</td><td>8.00</td><td>3.08</td><td class="grade">A</td><td class="signal-CORE">CORE</td></tr>
            <tr class="total"><td colspan="2">Total</td><td>$49,700</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>164.71</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p><em>Spread is shown from each listed player's own team perspective (negative = favorite). Implied team total = (Game Total − Team Spread) ÷ 2. "vs." labels the matchup only and doesn't indicate home/away. Injury blank = Active; none of this roster's players currently carry a Questionable/Doubtful/Out tag. Roster floor: ~114.50. Roster ceiling: ~231.40. $300 of cap space is unused.</em></p>
      <h3>What the Numbers Tell Us</h3>
      <p>The correlation is the story here, not any one player. New Orleans-Baltimore (Shough, Olave, Henry) is a genuine, deliberate 3-piece bring-back stack — the kind of construction the model's soft QB-stack preference is designed to produce. Layered on top of that, purely by coincidence of where the week's best remaining plays landed, Cincinnati-Houston (Chase Brown, Boutte) and Chicago-Minnesota (Swift, Vikings D/ST) each contribute a second correlated pair. Only Diggs and Kraft sit fully outside a shared-game connection. The practical effect: this build's outcome leans more heavily on three specific games going well than a typical Balanced roster would, even though nothing here was force-stacked beyond the Shough/Olave pairing.</p>
      <h3>DFS Translation</h3>
      <p><strong>How to read Grade and Signal:</strong> Signal layers Value (projected points per $1,000 of salary) on top of Grade to flag roster fit:</p>
      <ul class="dfs-list">
        <li><strong>CORE</strong> — Grade A and Value at or above a position-specific bar. Build your lineup around this player.</li>
        <li><strong>VALUE</strong> — Salary at or below a position-specific ceiling and Value at or above a bar. A cheap, efficient pivot that frees up cap space.</li>
        <li><strong>WATCH</strong> — Neither bar was cleared. Not a red flag, just no strong edge either way.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The exact cutoffs differ by position — a WR needs fewer raw points than a QB to earn a Grade A, for example:</p>
      <div class="roster-wrap">
        <table class="roster-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Grade A</th><th>Grade B</th><th>CORE — Value &ge;</th><th>VALUE — Salary &le;</th><th>VALUE — Value percentile &ge;</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="pos">QB</td><td>&ge; 20</td><td>&ge; 16</td><td>2.95</td><td>$6,500</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">RB</td><td>&ge; 18</td><td>&ge; 12</td><td>2.5</td><td>$5,800</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">WR</td><td>&ge; 16</td><td>&ge; 11</td><td>2.4</td><td>$5,200</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">TE</td><td>&ge; 15</td><td>&ge; 10</td><td>2.5</td><td>$3,700</td><td>65th</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pos">DST</td><td>&ge; 7.5</td><td>top 42% (58th pct)</td><td>2.4</td><td>$2,600</td><td>65th</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Below the Grade B line at each position is a Grade C; missing both the CORE and VALUE bars is a WATCH.</p>
      <p>For this build specifically:</p>
      <div class="translation-row">
        <div class="translation-pill core"><b>CORE</b>Derrick Henry, D'Andre Swift, Chris Olave, Kayshon Boutte, Vikings D/ST</div>
        <div class="translation-pill value"><b>VALUE</b>Tyler Shough</div>
        <div class="translation-pill watch"><b>WATCH</b>Stefon Diggs, Tucker Kraft, Chase Brown</div>
      </div>
      <p>Five CORE, one VALUE, three WATCH — a solid point-scoring foundation, though the concentration across three connected games is a real caveat for a build whose job is usually to differentiate.</p>
      <h3>The Slate-IQ Take</h3>
      <p>Worth being direct about the construction question first, since it's the headline: this build reads structurally closer to Leverage than to its own "close to Cash, 1-2 swaps" definition, purely because of how the correlations stacked up this week. That's not a flaw — Shough/Olave/Henry is a real, defensible stack, and the two incidental overlaps (Chase Brown/Boutte, Swift/Vikings) are strong individual plays that happen to share games rather than forced pairings. On the player level, Tucker Kraft and Stefon Diggs are the softest spots on the roster — both Grade B/WATCH with no standout signal — worth knowing that's where this build's floor is thinnest if something has to give.</p>
      <h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
      <p>A projection-driven build that ended up carrying real correlation across three separate games rather than the usual one-or-two-swap shape — the individual plays are sound, the connections are mostly a product of where this week's value landed rather than a deliberate strategy shift.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ GPP #1 ============ -->
<section class="lineup-section" id="gpp1">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="lineup-tag tag-gpp">GPP #1 · Tournament Build</span>
    <h2>Week 2 GPP Lineup: Panthers-Falcons &amp; Bengals-Texans Build</h2>
    <p class="lineup-excerpt">Two separate correlation clusters — a Carolina QB/WR stack with an Atlanta bring-back, plus a Cincinnati-Houston crossover through Xavier Hutchinson and Ja'Marr Chase — built around real ownership discrepancies rather than one big story.</p>
    <div class="lineup-stats-row">
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">$50,000</span><span class="l">Salary Used</span></div>
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">158.06</span><span class="l">Proj. Total</span></div>
      <div class="lineup-stat-pill"><span class="v">2</span><span class="l">Correlated Games</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <h3>Definition</h3>
      <p><strong>Leverage is built around ownership discrepancies, where the field is likely wrong.</strong> It gets 4 of the week's 15 $1 entries, paired one-for-one with a Ceiling entry into Three-Entry Max GPPs — the stack-focused tournament format built around high-upside game correlation. Construction-wise this is 2–4 player changes from Cash, stronger game correlation than Balanced, and more ownership differentiation — leverage-driven substitutions rather than a straight upgrade.</p>
      <h3>Why This Lineup, Why Now</h3>
      <p>This build carries two distinct correlation clusters rather than one. The first is a genuine Carolina stack: Bryce Young and Jalen Coker on the same Panthers offense, with Bijan Robinson as the bring-back on the Atlanta side of that same 43.5-point game — a real 3-piece, both-sides look. The second is a Cincinnati-Houston crossover: Xavier Hutchinson (Houston) and Ja'Marr Chase (Cincinnati) sit on opposite sides of the same 46.5-point game. Neither cluster was forced — both are the model's genuinely best available options at their salaries — but together they give this build more built-in correlation than Balanced while still leaving three standalone value plays (Christian Watson, Dallas Goedert, Jaguars D/ST) untouched by either stack.</p>
      <h3>The Lineup, Player by Player</h3>
      <p><strong>QB — Bryce Young ($5,400, CAR vs. ATL)</strong><br>Grade B, VALUE signal, projected for 19.97 points — 5th among 25 active QBs in raw points, 3rd in value. Carolina is a modest 1.5-point underdog in a competitive 43.5-point game, for a 22.5 implied team total — enough volume upside to anchor a real stack with Coker below at a below-market salary.</p>
      <p><strong>RB — Bijan Robinson ($8,200, ATL vs. CAR)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 25.16 points — 1st among 35 active RBs in raw points, 3rd in value. The bring-back half of this build's Carolina-Atlanta correlation, and independently the top raw-point RB on the entire board regardless of the stack, for a 21.0 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>RB — Ashton Jeanty ($6,800, LV vs. LAC)</strong><br>Grade A, WATCH signal, projected for 20.05 points — 4th among RBs in both raw points and value. A strong Grade A profile that hasn't cleared either Signal bar yet — the raw production is real, but the model isn't calling it a lock at this salary, for an 18.25 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Christian Watson ($6,200, GB vs. NYJ)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 19.08 points — 3rd among 39 active WRs in raw points and value alike. A standalone play with no correlation partner elsewhere on this roster — in purely on merit, same as it is in Cash, for a 24.0 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Jalen Coker ($5,100, CAR vs. ATL)</strong><br>Grade B, VALUE signal, projected for 15.30 points — 7th among WRs in raw points, 4th in value. The direct stack partner to Bryce Young on Carolina's side of the CAR@ATL game, for a 22.5 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>WR — Xavier Hutchinson ($3,500, HOU vs. CIN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 17.22 points — 4th among WRs in raw points, 2nd in value — the cheapest WR on this roster by a wide margin and the clearest single mispricing in the build. The Houston side of this build's second correlation cluster with Ja'Marr Chase, for a 24.75 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>TE — Dallas Goedert ($4,800, PHI vs. TEN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 15.39 points — 2nd among 27 active TEs in raw points and value. A standalone play, same profile that makes him a Cash-caliber CORE piece here too, for a 23.25 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>FLEX — Ja'Marr Chase ($7,600, CIN vs. HOU)</strong><br>Grade A, WATCH signal, projected for 16.53 points — 6th among WRs in raw points, but 18th in value — the most expensive piece on the roster and the one place this build pays up rather than leans on price. The Cincinnati side of the Hutchinson correlation; Grade A on production, but the salary keeps it off both Signal bars, for a 21.75 implied team total.</p>
      <p><strong>DST — Jacksonville Jaguars ($2,400, JAX vs. DEN)</strong><br>Grade A, CORE signal, projected for 9.38 points — 1st among 26 active DSTs in both raw points and value. A standalone play with no correlation tie elsewhere in this build — the same top-ranked unit anchoring Cash, for a 21.0 implied team total.</p>
      <h3>The Full Roster</h3>
      <div class="roster-wrap">
        <table class="roster-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Pos</th><th>Player</th><th>Salary</th><th>Matchup</th><th>Vegas (Total/Spread)</th><th>Implied Total</th><th>Proj. DK</th><th>Value ($/1K)</th><th>Grade</th><th>Signal</th></tr></thead>
