      <p><em>Spread is shown from each listed player's own team perspective (negative = favorite). Implied team total = (Game Total − Team Spread) ÷ 2. "vs." labels the matchup only and doesn't indicate home/away. Injury blank = Active; none of this roster's players currently carry a Questionable/Doubtful/Out tag. Roster floor: ~98.14. Roster ceiling: ~198.67. Full $50,000 cap spent.</em></p>
      <h3>What the Numbers Tell Us</h3>
      <p>Five of nine roster spots trace back to one game (Chicago-Minnesota, 49.5 total, both sides represented), with Chase Brown, McBride, and Wan'Dale Robinson diversifying into three other environments and Diggs adding a single-player look at the week's actual highest total (Washington-Dallas, 50.5). That's real, deliberate concentration risk — if Chicago-Minnesota turns into the shootout the total suggests, this roster has more ways to cash in than almost any build this week; if it doesn't, five roster spots are affected at once rather than one.</p>
      <h3>DFS Translation</h3>
      <p><strong>How to read Grade and Signal:</strong> Signal layers Value (projected points per $1,000 of salary) on top of Grade to flag roster fit:</p>
      <ul class="dfs-list">
        <li><strong>CORE</strong> — Grade A and Value at or above a position-specific bar. Build your lineup around this player.</li>
        <li><strong>VALUE</strong> — Salary at or below a position-specific ceiling and Value at or above a bar. A cheap, efficient pivot that frees up cap space.</li>
        <li><strong>WATCH</strong> — Neither bar was cleared. Not a red flag, just no strong edge either way.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The exact cutoffs differ by position — a WR needs fewer raw points than a QB to earn a Grade A, for example:</p>
      <div class="roster-wrap">
        <table class="roster-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Grade A</th><th>Grade B</th><th>CORE — Value &ge;</th><th>VALUE — Salary &le;</th><th>VALUE — Value percentile &ge;</th></tr></thead>
