      </div>
      <p>Below the Grade B line at each position is a Grade C; missing both the CORE and VALUE bars is a WATCH.</p>
      <p>For this build specifically:</p>
      <div class="translation-row">
        <div class="translation-pill core"><b>CORE</b>D'Andre Swift, Vikings D/ST</div>
        <div class="translation-pill value"><b>VALUE</b>Jordan Addison, Wan'Dale Robinson</div>
        <div class="translation-pill watch"><b>WATCH</b>Caleb Williams, Chase Brown, Rome Odunze, Stefon Diggs, Trey McBride</div>
      </div>
      <p>Two CORE, two VALUE, five WATCH — the fewest CORE-tier plays of the week's builds, a direct tradeoff for leaning this hard into one game's ceiling.</p>
      <h3>The Slate-IQ Take</h3>
      <p>The construction here is sound — a genuine two-sided bring-back around a top-tier total, not a forced stack — but it's worth being honest that this is a five-player bet on one game rather than four spread across the board. Caleb Williams and Trey McBride are both Grade A but sit at WATCH: Williams because his value rank trails several cheaper options even with the highest raw-point total among QBs, McBride because his salary ($6,900, the priciest non-RB on the roster) keeps him off the VALUE bar despite leading all TEs in raw production. Neither is a reason to avoid the build — just spots where "ceiling" means real upside that isn't fully locked in yet.</p>
      <h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
      <p>The highest-ceiling build of the week's four established lineups, anchored on real two-sided game correlation in the slate's 2nd-highest total environment rather than any one player's individual spike week — the tradeoff is that its outcome leans more heavily on a single game going well than any of the other builds.</p>
